%%entity DataConsent help: One informed decision by a client for one stated purpose and notice version. Keep separate records when purposes differ so withdrawing marketing consent never blocks regulated account servicing.
%%field DataConsent.client_id help: The client whose choice is recorded; downstream processing must check the matching purpose before using personal data.
%%field DataConsent.document_id help: The signed notice, recording or uploaded evidence that proves what the client saw when making the choice.
%%field DataConsent.purpose help: onboarding permits account setup; investment_advice supports planning; transaction_processing supports orders; regulatory_reporting covers mandatory disclosures; marketing and data_sharing remain independently withdrawable.
%%field DataConsent.status help: requested awaits a decision; granted permits the stated purpose; declined forbids it; withdrawn stops future discretionary use; expired requires renewal.
%%field DataConsent.notice_version help: The exact privacy notice wording presented, so later policy changes do not rewrite historical consent.
%%field DataConsent.requested_at help: When the client was asked, used to prove the notice preceded any consent-dependent processing.
%%field DataConsent.decided_at help: When the client granted, declined or withdrew the choice; empty while no decision has been made.
%%field DataConsent.valid_until help: The last date the decision may be relied on where the purpose or policy requires renewal.
%%field DataConsent.collection_channel help: Where the choice was captured, such as portal, paper, recorded call or branch desk.
%%field DataConsent.evidence_reference help: The immutable receipt, signature envelope or recording locator used during an audit or dispute.
%%field DataConsent.withdrawal_reason help: Why the client withdrew permission, retained to explain which future processing and communications must stop.
%%section entity: DataConsent; name: Decision; help: Who made the choice, for which purpose, against which notice, and whether it currently permits processing.; rows: client_id, purpose | status, notice_version
%%section entity: DataConsent; name: Evidence; help: When and where the choice was captured and the proof an auditor can retrieve.; rows: requested_at, decided_at | collection_channel, evidence_reference | document_id, valid_until
%%section entity: DataConsent; name: Withdrawal; help: Why an earlier permission ended; complete this only when the client withdraws it.; rows: withdrawal_reason; collapsed: true

%%entity BeneficialOwner help: A natural person who owns or ultimately controls an organizational client. The record supports KYC verification and must preserve former controllers rather than overwrite them.
%%field BeneficialOwner.organization_id help: The legal entity whose ownership or control chain is being established before onboarding or periodic review.
%%field BeneficialOwner.party_id help: The natural person identified as an ultimate owner or controller and subjected to identity and AML checks.
%%field BeneficialOwner.status help: declared is untested; verification_pending is being checked; verified may support onboarding; rejected is inconsistent or unsupported; ceased preserves a former controller.
%%field BeneficialOwner.ownership_percentage help: Direct and indirect economic ownership attributed to this person after tracing intermediate entities.
%%field BeneficialOwner.control_through_other_means help: Marks a controller identified through voting, appointment rights or influence rather than ownership percentage.
%%field BeneficialOwner.control_description help: How control is exercised when share ownership alone does not explain the determination.
%%field BeneficialOwner.effective_from help: When this ownership or control relationship began, used to reconstruct the KYC position at any past date.
%%field BeneficialOwner.effective_to help: When the relationship ended; leave empty while the person remains an owner or controller.
%%field BeneficialOwner.verified_on help: When evidence was last checked; periodic review uses this date to identify stale ownership declarations.
%%section entity: BeneficialOwner; name: Controller; help: The organization, natural person and basis on which ultimate control has been attributed.; rows: organization_id, party_id | ownership_percentage, control_through_other_means | control_description
%%section entity: BeneficialOwner; name: Verification; help: Where the determination stands and the period for which it is supported by evidence.; rows: status, verified_on | effective_from, effective_to

%%entity AMLScreening help: One sanctions, politically exposed person, adverse-media or watch-list screening attempt. Re-screening creates another record so changes in lists and provider results remain provable.
%%field AMLScreening.client_id help: The client relationship being assessed; a confirmed match can suspend onboarding, advice and transaction execution.
%%field AMLScreening.party_id help: The person or organization name submitted to the screening provider for matching.
%%field AMLScreening.status help: queued and screening are incomplete; clear permits ordinary processing; potential_match needs human review; confirmed_match requires escalation; error must be rerun.
%%field AMLScreening.screening_type help: The control performed, such as sanctions, PEP, adverse media or internal watch-list screening.
%%field AMLScreening.provider help: The service or internal list that returned the result, needed to reproduce the check.
%%field AMLScreening.screened_at help: When the provider result was obtained, which determines which list version and client facts applied.
%%field AMLScreening.provider_reference help: The external result identifier used to retrieve match candidates and evidence without copying restricted data.
%%field AMLScreening.match_score help: The provider's similarity measure; it is a triage aid and never substitutes for an investigator's decision.
%%field AMLScreening.match_summary help: The minimum facts explaining why a candidate matched and what must be compared by compliance.
%%field AMLScreening.next_screening_date help: When periodic monitoring must run again if no event-driven re-screening occurs first.
%%section entity: AMLScreening; name: Subject; help: The client and party checked, the control used, and the provider that performed it.; rows: client_id, party_id | screening_type, provider
%%section entity: AMLScreening; name: Result; help: The returned state, timing and match information that determines whether investigation is required.; rows: status, screened_at | match_score, provider_reference | match_summary
%%section entity: AMLScreening; name: Monitoring; help: The next routine check due after this result remains valid.; rows: next_screening_date

%%entity ComplianceCase help: The controlled investigation of an AML, KYC, conduct or regulatory concern. It separates machine-generated alerts from the documented human decision and any filing.
%%field ComplianceCase.client_id help: The client relationship affected by the concern and any restrictions imposed while it is investigated.
%%field ComplianceCase.aml_screening_id help: The screening result that opened the case, when the concern originated in name matching.
%%field ComplianceCase.advisor_id help: The accountable staff owner coordinating evidence and deadlines; this does not make the advisor the subject.
%%field ComplianceCase.case_number help: The stable internal reference quoted in approvals, filings and audit correspondence.
%%field ComplianceCase.case_type help: The regulatory concern being handled, such as AML match, KYC discrepancy, suitability exception or conduct review.
%%field ComplianceCase.status help: opened awaits triage; investigating gathers evidence; awaiting_information is blocked; escalated needs senior review; reported has gone to an authority; cleared or closed are terminal.
%%field ComplianceCase.priority help: The response urgency based on legal deadline, client impact and risk rather than commercial value.
%%field ComplianceCase.opened_at help: When the concern became a managed case and regulatory response clocks began.
%%field ComplianceCase.due_at help: The internal deadline set early enough to meet the applicable external reporting or remediation limit.
%%field ComplianceCase.closed_at help: When all investigation, filing and remediation work finished; empty while any obligation remains.
%%field ComplianceCase.decision help: The investigator's outcome, such as false positive, monitor, restrict, exit or report.
%%field ComplianceCase.rationale help: The evidence-based reasoning that lets an independent reviewer understand and reproduce the decision.
%%section entity: ComplianceCase; name: Case; help: The reference, concern, affected client, owner and current point in the investigation.; rows: case_number | case_type, priority | client_id, advisor_id | aml_screening_id
%%section entity: ComplianceCase; name: Timelines; help: When the case began, when action is due, and when every obligation was completed.; rows: opened_at, due_at | closed_at
%%section entity: ComplianceCase; name: Decision; help: The final outcome and evidence-based reasoning retained for regulatory review.; rows: decision | rationale

%%entity ClientAgreement help: A versioned contract governing advisory or portfolio-management services, fees, conflicts and termination. A new signed version supersedes rather than overwrites the old one.
%%field ClientAgreement.client_id help: The client legally bound by these service terms and fee arrangements.
%%field ClientAgreement.fee_schedule_id help: The agreed pricing schedule; invoices must reference the same terms unless an amendment is signed.
%%field ClientAgreement.document_id help: The executed document retained as evidence of the clauses and disclosures accepted.
%%field ClientAgreement.agreement_number help: The unique reference used in invoices, service records and regulator correspondence.
%%field ClientAgreement.agreement_type help: The regulated service covered, such as investment advice, distribution or portfolio management.
%%field ClientAgreement.status help: draft is editable; issued awaits signature; signed awaits its effective date; active governs service; superseded, terminated and expired are historical.
%%field ClientAgreement.version_number help: The business version printed on the agreement so amendments can be traced without changing prior terms.
%%field ClientAgreement.issued_date help: When this version was sent to the client for review and signature.
%%field ClientAgreement.signed_date help: When all required parties completed execution; service must not start earlier where signature is mandatory.
%%field ClientAgreement.effective_date help: The first day this version governs the relationship, fees and obligations.
%%field ClientAgreement.expiry_date help: The last scheduled day of the agreement when it is not open-ended.
%%field ClientAgreement.termination_reason help: Why the relationship ended, retained to distinguish expiry, client exit, compliance exit and breach.
%%section entity: ClientAgreement; name: Agreement; help: The client, regulated service, reference, version and current legal standing.; rows: agreement_number, status | client_id, agreement_type | version_number
%%section entity: ClientAgreement; name: Terms and evidence; help: The agreed fee basis and executed document that prove what the client accepted.; rows: fee_schedule_id, document_id
%%section entity: ClientAgreement; name: Effective period; help: When this version was issued, signed, effective and no longer applicable.; rows: issued_date, signed_date | effective_date, expiry_date | termination_reason

%%entity ConflictDisclosure help: A potential or actual conflict involving an adviser, client or product, with the mitigation and disclosure required before advice proceeds.
%%field ConflictDisclosure.client_id help: The client whose interests may be affected; empty only for a firm-wide conflict.
%%field ConflictDisclosure.advisor_id help: The adviser or accountable employee connected to the conflict and responsible for following the mitigation.
%%field ConflictDisclosure.security_id help: The product involved when remuneration, ownership or issuer relationships create the conflict.
%%field ConflictDisclosure.conflict_type help: The source of competing interest, such as commission, personal holding, related party, referral or product bias.
%%field ConflictDisclosure.status help: identified awaits review; under_review assesses materiality; mitigated has controls; disclosed has reached the client; accepted permits documented continuation; prohibited stops the activity; closed is historical.
%%field ConflictDisclosure.identified_date help: When the firm first knew or should have known of the competing interest.
%%field ConflictDisclosure.description help: The facts creating the conflict, written so a reviewer can judge materiality without relying on a category code.
%%field ConflictDisclosure.mitigation help: The control applied, such as recusal, independent review, fee rebate, product exclusion or information barrier.
%%field ConflictDisclosure.disclosed_at help: When the client received clear disclosure before making the affected decision.
%%field ConflictDisclosure.accepted_at help: When the client acknowledged the disclosure where informed continuation is permitted.
%%section entity: ConflictDisclosure; name: Conflict; help: Who and what are affected, the source of competing interest, and its present disposition.; rows: conflict_type, status | client_id, advisor_id | security_id, identified_date | description
%%section entity: ConflictDisclosure; name: Control and disclosure; help: How the conflict was neutralized or explained before the client proceeded.; rows: mitigation | disclosed_at, accepted_at

%%entity FeeInvoice help: A client charge calculated under a signed agreement and fee schedule. It separates advisory revenue owed directly by the client from product commission.
%%field FeeInvoice.client_id help: The client responsible for the invoice and entitled to a transparent calculation.
%%field FeeInvoice.fee_schedule_id help: The pricing rule used to derive the charge, allowing finance and compliance to reproduce it.
%%field FeeInvoice.agreement_id help: The signed agreement authorizing the service and fee basis for this billing period.
%%field FeeInvoice.invoice_number help: The unique reference printed on the invoice and used when matching receipts or disputes.
%%field FeeInvoice.status help: draft is unissued; issued is delivered; due awaits payment; partially_paid has a balance; paid is settled; disputed needs review; waived and cancelled end collection; overdue has missed its due date.
%%field FeeInvoice.invoice_date help: The accounting and communication date on which the charge was formally raised.
%%field FeeInvoice.due_date help: The promised payment deadline used for reminders and overdue escalation.
%%field FeeInvoice.gross_amount help: The service fee before tax, rebates, credits or prior payments.
%%field FeeInvoice.tax_amount help: The indirect tax charged under the applicable invoice rules.
%%field FeeInvoice.net_amount help: The total amount payable after tax and adjustments; it must reconcile to the invoice document.
%%field FeeInvoice.paid_amount help: The money matched to the invoice so far, supporting partial-payment and outstanding-balance reporting.
%%field FeeInvoice.paid_date help: When the invoice became fully settled; empty while any balance remains.
%%field FeeInvoice.dispute_reason help: The client's stated objection to amount, service period or fee basis and the starting point for resolution.
%%section entity: FeeInvoice; name: Invoice; help: The client, agreement, pricing basis, reference and collection state for the charge.; rows: invoice_number, status | client_id, agreement_id | fee_schedule_id
%%section entity: FeeInvoice; name: Amounts and dates; help: What was charged, the tax applied, what has been paid and when the balance is due.; rows: invoice_date, due_date | gross_amount, tax_amount | net_amount, paid_amount | paid_date
%%section entity: FeeInvoice; name: Dispute; help: Why collection has paused while the client challenges the charge.; rows: dispute_reason; collapsed: true

%%entity InvestorComplaint help: A client grievance managed from receipt through response and escalation, including the external SCORES reference when applicable.
%%field InvestorComplaint.client_id help: The complainant whose service, advice, transaction or data issue must be investigated.
%%field InvestorComplaint.advisor_id help: The accountable handler coordinating the response; it need not be the person complained about.
%%field InvestorComplaint.complaint_number help: The internal reference supplied in every acknowledgement, response and escalation.
%%field InvestorComplaint.channel help: Where the grievance arrived, such as branch, email, telephone, portal or SCORES.
%%field InvestorComplaint.category help: The business area complained about, used to route ownership and identify systemic patterns.
%%field InvestorComplaint.priority help: The urgency set from financial harm, vulnerability, legal deadline and reputational impact.
%%field InvestorComplaint.status help: received awaits acknowledgement; acknowledged has entered the response clock; investigating gathers facts; awaiting_client is blocked; resolved or rejected has a decision; escalated is externally or internally reviewed; closed confirms completion.
%%field InvestorComplaint.received_at help: When any part of the firm first received the grievance, which starts the response timetable.
%%field InvestorComplaint.acknowledged_at help: When the client was told the complaint reference, owner and expected response route.
%%field InvestorComplaint.response_due_at help: The internal deadline aligned to the applicable redressal period and escalation buffer.
%%field InvestorComplaint.resolved_at help: When the final response and remedy were completed, not merely drafted.
%%field InvestorComplaint.scores_reference help: The external SEBI complaint reference where the grievance is lodged or escalated through SCORES.
%%field InvestorComplaint.summary help: The client's issue in neutral language, including the service, date and harm alleged.
%%field InvestorComplaint.resolution help: The findings, remedy, rejection grounds and further escalation rights communicated to the client.
%%section entity: InvestorComplaint; name: Complaint; help: The complainant, reference, channel, issue category, handler and current response state.; rows: complaint_number | client_id, advisor_id | channel, category | priority, scores_reference | summary
%%section entity: InvestorComplaint; name: Response clock; help: Receipt, acknowledgement, deadline and completion dates used to prevent silent overdue grievances.; rows: received_at, acknowledged_at | response_due_at, resolved_at
%%section entity: InvestorComplaint; name: Outcome; help: The investigated response, remedy and escalation information delivered to the client.; rows: resolution

%%entity RegulatoryFiling help: One statutory or supervisory submission, its approval, delivery and regulator acknowledgement. Corrections remain linked through the stable filing number and period.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.compliance_case_id help: The investigation requiring the filing when submission is event-driven rather than periodic.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.document_id help: The approved return, report or evidence package exactly as submitted.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.filing_number help: The firm's unique reference connecting preparation, approval, transmission and acknowledgement.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.regulator help: The authority or reporting utility receiving the submission, such as SEBI, FIU-IND or another competent body.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.filing_type help: The return or notification required, including periodic reports, suspicious-transaction reports, breach notices or complaint reports.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.reporting_period help: The date range or event reference covered, preventing duplicate or missing submissions.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.status help: preparing is editable; pending_approval awaits sign-off; approved may be transmitted; submitted awaits response; accepted is complete; rejected or correction_required must be fixed.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.due_date help: The legal submission deadline; internal approval should be scheduled before it.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.submitted_at help: When the payload left the firm's control and was sent to the named authority.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.accepted_at help: When the authority acknowledged successful receipt or validation.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.acknowledgement_reference help: The regulator's receipt identifier proving timely and complete submission.
%%field RegulatoryFiling.rejection_reason help: The validation or content problem that must be corrected before the filing can be accepted.
%%section entity: RegulatoryFiling; name: Filing; help: The authority, return, period, case and internal reference that identify the obligation.; rows: filing_number | regulator, filing_type | reporting_period, due_date | compliance_case_id, document_id
%%section entity: RegulatoryFiling; name: Submission; help: When the filing was sent and accepted and the acknowledgement that proves completion.; rows: submitted_at, accepted_at | acknowledgement_reference | rejection_reason

%%entity ComplianceBreach help: A confirmed or suspected failure of law, policy, control or service obligation, managed through containment, investigation, notification and remediation.
%%field ComplianceBreach.client_id help: The affected client when the breach concerns one relationship; empty for a firm-wide event.
%%field ComplianceBreach.advisor_id help: The accountable remediation owner, not necessarily the person who caused the failure.
%%field ComplianceBreach.external_integration_id help: The connected system involved where failed synchronization, leakage or incorrect data caused the event.
%%field ComplianceBreach.breach_number help: The immutable reference used across incident meetings, notifications, filings and corrective actions.
%%field ComplianceBreach.breach_type help: The obligation or control that failed, such as privacy, AML, suitability, conduct, reporting or cybersecurity.
%%field ComplianceBreach.severity help: low has limited effect; medium needs managed correction; high risks material client or regulatory harm; critical requires immediate executive and regulator response.
%%field ComplianceBreach.status help: detected awaits containment; contained limits further harm; investigating finds scope and cause; remediating applies correction; reported has reached authorities; resolved completes action; closed confirms review.
%%field ComplianceBreach.detected_at help: When the firm first identified the failure, which starts incident and notification clocks.
%%field ComplianceBreach.contained_at help: When immediate steps stopped continuing exposure or harm.
%%field ComplianceBreach.reported_at help: When required internal, client or regulatory notifications were completed.
%%field ComplianceBreach.resolved_at help: When remediation was verified effective rather than merely assigned.
%%field ComplianceBreach.description help: What happened, which records and people were affected, and the observed or potential harm.
%%field ComplianceBreach.root_cause help: The underlying process, system, control or human cause established after investigation.
%%field ComplianceBreach.remediation help: Corrective and preventive actions, including ownership and evidence of effectiveness.
%%section entity: ComplianceBreach; name: Incident; help: The reference, kind, severity, affected relationship, responsible owner and current response state.; rows: breach_number | breach_type, severity | client_id, advisor_id | external_integration_id | description
%%section entity: ComplianceBreach; name: Response timeline; help: Detection, containment, notification and verified resolution dates for the incident clock.; rows: detected_at, contained_at | reported_at, resolved_at
%%section entity: ComplianceBreach; name: Cause and remediation; help: Why the failure occurred and the corrective action that prevents recurrence.; rows: root_cause | remediation

%%entity RetentionPolicy help: An approved rule stating how long a category of records is preserved, which event starts the period, and why destruction is lawful.
%%field RetentionPolicy.name help: The recognizable policy title used when assigning legal holds and reviewing disposal decisions.
%%field RetentionPolicy.record_category help: The business records governed, such as KYC evidence, advice, orders, complaints, invoices or audit events.
%%field RetentionPolicy.status help: draft awaits approval; active governs records; suspended prevents disposal; superseded preserves history; retired no longer applies to new records.
%%field RetentionPolicy.retention_years help: The minimum full years records remain available after the trigger event, subject to any longer legal hold.
%%field RetentionPolicy.trigger_event help: The event from which the period runs, such as account closure, transaction date, case closure or agreement termination.
%%field RetentionPolicy.legal_basis help: The regulation, contract, limitation period or defensible business need supporting retention.
%%field RetentionPolicy.effective_date help: The first day this version governs new retention calculations.
%%field RetentionPolicy.review_date help: When compliance must confirm that law, business need and disposal controls remain current.
%%field RetentionPolicy.destruction_requires_approval help: Whether a second person must approve disposal after retention expires and no hold remains.
%%section entity: RetentionPolicy; name: Policy; help: The record class, status, duration, trigger and legal reason controlling preservation.; rows: name, status | record_category, retention_years | trigger_event | legal_basis
%%section entity: RetentionPolicy; name: Governance; help: When the rule applies, when it must be reviewed and whether destruction needs dual control.; rows: effective_date, review_date | destruction_requires_approval

%%entity LegalHold help: A temporary instruction preventing disposal of records relevant to an investigation, complaint, litigation or regulator request, regardless of normal retention expiry.
%%field LegalHold.client_id help: The client whose records must be preserved when the hold is relationship-specific.
%%field LegalHold.retention_policy_id help: The ordinary retention rule being overridden while the hold remains active.
%%field LegalHold.compliance_case_id help: The investigation or regulatory concern requiring preservation and defining eventual release.
%%field LegalHold.hold_number help: The unique reference communicated to custodians and used to prove the preservation instruction.
%%field LegalHold.status help: proposed awaits authorization; active blocks disposal; released resumes ordinary retention calculation; cancelled records a hold that never took effect.
%%field LegalHold.reason help: The matter and preservation need stated without exposing privileged investigative detail unnecessarily.
%%field LegalHold.imposed_at help: When disposal must stop across all systems and physical repositories in scope.
%%field LegalHold.released_at help: When authorized release ended the hold after the matter and appeal windows closed.
%%field LegalHold.scope help: The record types, people, date range, systems and custodians covered by the preservation instruction.
%%field LegalHold.authority_reference help: The court order, regulator request, legal instruction or internal authorization supporting the hold.
%%section entity: LegalHold; name: Hold; help: The reference, state, client, case and policy identifying what overrides normal disposal.; rows: hold_number, status | client_id, compliance_case_id | retention_policy_id
%%section entity: LegalHold; name: Preservation; help: Why, when and across which records the hold prevents destruction.; rows: imposed_at, released_at | reason | scope | authority_reference

