%%report fee-invoice-largest-by-tax-amount title: The ten largest fee invoices by tax amount entity: FeeInvoice chart: bar x: record y: tax_amount help: A client charge calculated under a signed agreement and fee schedule. The records with the highest tax amount; worth reading for outliers and for the few that carry most of the total. sql: SELECT invoice_number::text AS record, tax_amount FROM bus_fee_invoice WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND tax_amount IS NOT NULL ORDER BY tax_amount DESC LIMIT 10
%%report fee-invoice-net-amount-by-status title: Total net amount by status entity: FeeInvoice chart: bar x: status y: total_net_amount help: A client charge calculated under a signed agreement and fee schedule. Fee Invoices added up on net amount, split by status, to show where the quantity sits. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, ROUND(SUM(net_amount)::numeric, 2) AS total_net_amount FROM bus_fee_invoice WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST
%%report fee-invoice-net-amount-per-month title: Average net amount per month entity: FeeInvoice chart: line x: month y: average_net_amount help: A client charge calculated under a signed agreement and fee schedule. The average net amount of fee invoices created each month, to see whether the typical record is growing or shrinking. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, ROUND(AVG(net_amount)::numeric, 2) AS average_net_amount FROM bus_fee_invoice WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
%%report fee-invoice-largest-by-net-amount title: The ten largest fee invoices by net amount entity: FeeInvoice chart: bar x: record y: net_amount help: A client charge calculated under a signed agreement and fee schedule. The records with the highest net amount; worth reading for outliers and for the few that carry most of the total. sql: SELECT invoice_number::text AS record, net_amount FROM bus_fee_invoice WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND net_amount IS NOT NULL ORDER BY net_amount DESC LIMIT 10
%%report fee-invoice-per-client title: Fee Invoices per client entity: FeeInvoice chart: bar x: client y: records help: A client charge calculated under a signed agreement and fee schedule. Which client the most fee invoices belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.client_number::text, '(none)') AS client, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_fee_invoice x LEFT JOIN bus_client p ON p.id = x.client_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report fee-invoice-gross-amount-per-client title: Total gross amount per client entity: FeeInvoice chart: bar x: client y: total_gross_amount help: A client charge calculated under a signed agreement and fee schedule. Fee Invoices added up on gross amount for each client, ten largest first. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.client_number::text, '(none)') AS client, ROUND(SUM(x.gross_amount)::numeric, 2) AS total_gross_amount FROM bus_fee_invoice x LEFT JOIN bus_client p ON p.id = x.client_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST LIMIT 10
%%report fee-invoice-top-dispute-reason title: Fee Invoices: the most common dispute reason entity: FeeInvoice chart: bar x: dispute_reason y: records help: A client charge calculated under a signed agreement and fee schedule. The ten most frequent values of dispute reason among fee invoices; free text that repeats is a list the model could close. sql: SELECT COALESCE(dispute_reason, '(blank)') AS dispute_reason, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_fee_invoice WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report fee-invoice-completeness title: How complete fee invoices are entity: FeeInvoice chart: bar x: field y: missing help: A client charge calculated under a signed agreement and fee schedule. For each optional field, how many fee invoices leave it empty; the long bars are the fields nobody fills in. sql: SELECT 'tax amount' AS field, COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE tax_amount IS NULL) AS missing FROM bus_fee_invoice WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'paid amount', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE paid_amount IS NULL) FROM bus_fee_invoice WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'paid date', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE paid_date IS NULL) FROM bus_fee_invoice WHERE deleted_at IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT 'dispute reason', COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE dispute_reason IS NULL) FROM bus_fee_invoice WHERE deleted_at IS NULL ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report investor-complaint-by-status title: Investor Complaints by status entity: InvestorComplaint chart: bar x: status y: records help: A client grievance managed from receipt through response and escalation, including the external SCORES reference when applicable. How investor complaints divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_investor_complaint WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report investor-complaint-lifecycle title: Investor Complaint lifecycle entity: InvestorComplaint chart: bar x: state y: records help: A client grievance managed from receipt through response and escalation, including the external SCORES reference when applicable. Where investor complaints sit in their state machine, in the order the diagram draws it; a state nothing has reached shows as zero. sql: SELECT d.state, COALESCE(c.records, 0) AS records FROM (SELECT 'received' AS state, 0 AS position UNION ALL SELECT 'acknowledged', 1 UNION ALL SELECT 'investigating', 2 UNION ALL SELECT 'awaiting_client', 3 UNION ALL SELECT 'resolved', 4 UNION ALL SELECT 'rejected', 5 UNION ALL SELECT 'escalated', 6 UNION ALL SELECT 'closed', 7) d LEFT JOIN (SELECT status AS state, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_investor_complaint WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1) c ON c.state = d.state ORDER BY d.position
%%report investor-complaint-per-client title: Investor Complaints per client entity: InvestorComplaint chart: bar x: client y: records help: A client grievance managed from receipt through response and escalation, including the external SCORES reference when applicable. Which client the most investor complaints belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.client_number::text, '(none)') AS client, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_investor_complaint x LEFT JOIN bus_client p ON p.id = x.client_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report investor-complaint-top-channel title: Investor Complaints: the most common channel entity: InvestorComplaint chart: bar x: channel y: records help: A client grievance managed from receipt through response and escalation, including the external SCORES reference when applicable. The ten most frequent values of channel among investor complaints; free text that repeats is a list the model could close. sql: SELECT COALESCE(channel, '(blank)') AS channel, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_investor_complaint WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report investor-complaint-top-category title: Investor Complaints: the most common category entity: InvestorComplaint chart: bar x: category y: records help: A client grievance managed from receipt through response and escalation, including the external SCORES reference when applicable. The ten most frequent values of category among investor complaints; free text that repeats is a list the model could close. sql: SELECT COALESCE(category, '(blank)') AS category, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_investor_complaint WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report regulatory-filing-by-status title: Regulatory Filings by status entity: RegulatoryFiling chart: bar x: status y: records help: One statutory or supervisory submission, its approval, delivery and regulator acknowledgement. How regulatory filings divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_regulatory_filing WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report regulatory-filing-lifecycle title: Regulatory Filing lifecycle entity: RegulatoryFiling chart: bar x: state y: records help: One statutory or supervisory submission, its approval, delivery and regulator acknowledgement. Where regulatory filings sit in their state machine, in the order the diagram draws it; a state nothing has reached shows as zero. sql: SELECT d.state, COALESCE(c.records, 0) AS records FROM (SELECT 'preparing' AS state, 0 AS position UNION ALL SELECT 'pending_approval', 1 UNION ALL SELECT 'approved', 2 UNION ALL SELECT 'submitted', 3 UNION ALL SELECT 'accepted', 4 UNION ALL SELECT 'rejected', 5 UNION ALL SELECT 'correction_required', 6) d LEFT JOIN (SELECT status AS state, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_regulatory_filing WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1) c ON c.state = d.state ORDER BY d.position
%%report regulatory-filing-top-filing-type title: Regulatory Filings: the most common filing type entity: RegulatoryFiling chart: bar x: filing_type y: records help: One statutory or supervisory submission, its approval, delivery and regulator acknowledgement. The ten most frequent values of filing type among regulatory filings; free text that repeats is a list the model could close. sql: SELECT COALESCE(filing_type, '(blank)') AS filing_type, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_regulatory_filing WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report compliance-breach-by-status title: Compliance Breaches by status entity: ComplianceBreach chart: bar x: status y: records help: A confirmed or suspected failure of law, policy, control or service obligation, managed through containment, investigation, notification and remediation. How compliance breaches divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_compliance_breach WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report compliance-breach-by-severity title: Compliance Breaches by severity entity: ComplianceBreach chart: pie x: severity y: records help: A confirmed or suspected failure of law, policy, control or service obligation, managed through containment, investigation, notification and remediation. How compliance breaches divide across severity, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(severity::text, '(unset)') AS severity, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_compliance_breach WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report compliance-breach-lifecycle title: Compliance Breach lifecycle entity: ComplianceBreach chart: bar x: state y: records help: A confirmed or suspected failure of law, policy, control or service obligation, managed through containment, investigation, notification and remediation. Where compliance breaches sit in their state machine, in the order the diagram draws it; a state nothing has reached shows as zero. sql: SELECT d.state, COALESCE(c.records, 0) AS records FROM (SELECT 'detected' AS state, 0 AS position UNION ALL SELECT 'contained', 1 UNION ALL SELECT 'investigating', 2 UNION ALL SELECT 'remediating', 3 UNION ALL SELECT 'reported', 4 UNION ALL SELECT 'resolved', 5 UNION ALL SELECT 'closed', 6) d LEFT JOIN (SELECT status AS state, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_compliance_breach WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1) c ON c.state = d.state ORDER BY d.position
%%report retention-policy-by-status title: Retention Policies by status entity: RetentionPolicy chart: pie x: status y: records help: An approved rule stating how long a category of records is preserved, which event starts the period, and why destruction is lawful. How retention policies divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_retention_policy WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report retention-policy-retention-years-by-status title: Total retention years by status entity: RetentionPolicy chart: bar x: status y: total_retention_years help: An approved rule stating how long a category of records is preserved, which event starts the period, and why destruction is lawful. Retention Policies added up on retention years, split by status, to show where the quantity sits. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, ROUND(SUM(retention_years)::numeric, 2) AS total_retention_years FROM bus_retention_policy WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC NULLS LAST
%%report retention-policy-top-record-category title: Retention Policies: the most common record category entity: RetentionPolicy chart: bar x: record_category y: records help: An approved rule stating how long a category of records is preserved, which event starts the period, and why destruction is lawful. The ten most frequent values of record category among retention policies; free text that repeats is a list the model could close. sql: SELECT COALESCE(record_category, '(blank)') AS record_category, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_retention_policy WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report legal-hold-by-status title: Legal Holds by status entity: LegalHold chart: pie x: status y: records help: A temporary instruction preventing disposal of records relevant to an investigation, complaint, litigation or regulator request, regardless of normal retention expiry. How legal holds divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_legal_hold WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report legal-hold-top-reason title: Legal Holds: the most common reason entity: LegalHold chart: bar x: reason y: records help: A temporary instruction preventing disposal of records relevant to an investigation, complaint, litigation or regulator request, regardless of normal retention expiry. The ten most frequent values of reason among legal holds; free text that repeats is a list the model could close. sql: SELECT COALESCE(reason, '(blank)') AS reason, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_legal_hold WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
